gm-method/rendering: an adaptive-quality controller that drops/raises resolution via renderer.setPixelRatio() will FLASH any FBO-backed composite effect (a half-res water/atmosphere pass, bloom, SSAO) to blank for one frame on EVERY toggle, because setPixelRatio resizes the drawing buffer and the effect reallocs its render target (texImage2D with null = uninitialized) on the next frame. The user sees 'X flashes out of view' ~once per toggle. Exponential dwell backoff reduces the FREQUENCY of toggles but cannot make a single toggle not flash. Before rewiring the controller onto a 'hitch-free' resolution dial (one that only changes the viewport upscale SOURCE RECT with no realloc), VERIFY LIVE that the dial is actually EFFECTIVE at the pose the user plays at -- such dials are often gated to a specific altitude/condition (e.g. a half-res-water path active only at deck/space distance) and are INERT at the ground gameplay pose, so routing the controller through them silently no-ops. If no realloc-free resolution lever exists at the gameplay pose, the correct fix is to REMOVE the setPixelRatio drop entirely (fixed pixelRatio = no resize = no realloc = no flash, ever) rather than keep a controller that fights a losing battle on slow devices AND flashes. WITNESS METHOD: read the live controller debug state (engaged/pixelRatio/reDrops) to confirm it toggled; toggle the candidate dial live and check the effect's quad/draw count CHANGES (inert if constant); after the fix assert pixelRatio is stable over seconds and the composite keeps rendering. The headless surface often has devicePixelRatio=1 so setPixelRatio(1.0) is a no-op there and CANNOT reproduce the flash -- it needs DPR>1 (the user's real machine).